      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Introduction and “Playing the crowd”, “Beyond the gates”, “Reaching east”: Fortunatus and Baudonivia, the donations (Vita I.12–13), the Rule and communal gifts, and the eastern mission (Vita II.16)."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:gregory-radegund-funeral-attestation-933",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Attestation 933, Gregory, Glory of the Confessors 104: he went to Radegund’s community at Poitiers on hearing of her death."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:chevalier-radegonde-caen",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Notice pddn_hagio.202110061928562430200, Mort and Éléments d’identification: Radegund died at Poitiers in August 587."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:gregory-poitiers-nuns-at-tours-ix-39",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "IX.39, opening: Clotild, Basina, the group’s departure from Poitiers and appeal to Gregory in Tours, followed by the bishop’s version of his answer."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:gregory-poitiers-episcopal-verdict-x-16",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "X.16, pp. 123–126: complaints, Leubovera’s answers, Didimia confirming her gift to the abbess and the judges’ reasons for retaining her."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:gregory-histories-krusch-levison",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "X.16, from De palla quod repotarent to donatrix Didimia confirmavit: a silk wrap brought de parentibus (from her kin) was given to the abbess. The account distinguishes the original wrap, the altar covering and the trim."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:flierman-gregory-and-the-merovingian-letter",
      "role": "supports",
